🎯 Intent

Enable authenticated users to save and retrieve scenarios they have explored, creating continuity across sessions — without introducing deal semantics, commitment signals, or property requirements.

Scenario persistence exists to support:

memory

iteration

learning

Not:

offers

deals

approvals

submissions

🧭 Product Context (Non-Negotiable)

In Sprint 2:

A Scenario is:

pre-deal

persona-bound

editable

non-binding

optionally property-less

A Deal does not exist yet as a first-class object.

Persistence must not imply:

seriousness

finality

readiness to transact

🔒 UX & Semantic Constraints (Hard Rules)

Scenario persistence must:

Be available only to authenticated users

Preserve deterministic scenario summaries

Allow multiple scenarios per user

Preserve informal history (chronological)

Scenario persistence must not:

Introduce Deal IDs

Require Property IDs

Introduce status states (draft / submitted / approved)

Introduce sharing or collaboration

Introduce binding language (“final”, “sent”, “locked”)

If unsure → do not add it.

🛠️ Implementation Scope
Required

Persist scenario data to app database

Associate scenarios with authenticated user

Store:

persona

raw inputs (JSON)

generated scenario summary (string)

timestamps

Support retrieval of scenarios by user

Explicitly Out of Scope

Scenario editing UI (handled in S2-003 / S2-004)

CRM sync on update (handled later)

Deal creation

Property verification

🔀 Behavioral Rules

Scenario creation:

Creates a new scenario record

Does not overwrite prior scenarios

History:

Chronological

Informal

Narrative, not legal

✅ Acceptance Criteria (All Must Pass)

 Authenticated users can save scenarios

 Users can have multiple scenarios

 Scenario summary matches deterministic output

 Scenarios are retrievable by the same user

 Scenarios do not require property IDs

 No deal or submission language introduced

 No breaking changes to Sprint 1 behavior

